# libarchive -- flatpak's hard dependency for OCI layers and .flatpak bundles.
#
# flatpak's meson takes dependency('libarchive', version: '>=2.8.0') with no
# option guard. ostree can also use it, behind --with-libarchive.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/libarchive/libarchive/releases/download/v3.8.1/libarchive-3.8.1.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=19f917d42d530f98815ac824d90c7eaf648e9d9a50e4f309c812457ffa5496b5
SRC_DIR=libarchive-3.8.1

# READ THE DEPENDENCY LIST IN TAPEBUILD.toml AS THE SPECIFICATION, NOT AS A
# DESCRIPTION. That is unusual and it is the whole point of this comment.
#
# libarchive's compression and crypto backends have NO --with- FLAGS AT ALL.
# configure probes for them by linking a test program, exactly as zstd's Makefile
# does, and there is no variable to pin:
#     ac_cv_lib_z_       ac_cv_lib_bz2_     ac_cv_lib_lzma_
#     ac_cv_lib_zstd_    ac_cv_lib_crypto_  ac_cv_lib_xml2_
# So WHICH FORMATS THIS LIBRARY CAN READ IS DECIDED ENTIRELY BY WHAT IS PRESENT
# IN THE BUILD ENVIRONMENT. There is no flag to force a backend on and none to
# force one off.
#
# The consequence is the inverse of every other recipe here. Normally
# [dependencies] records what a package needs; here it DETERMINES WHAT THE
# PACKAGE BECOMES, because tape seeds exactly what is declared and configure
# links exactly what it finds. An under-declared dependency does not fail the
# build -- it produces a libarchive silently missing a format, and the failure
# appears later as an archive that "is corrupt" to a program that simply cannot
# decompress it.
#
# So all eight are declared deliberately and none of them is incidental:
#   zlib, bzip2, xz, zstd   the four compression backends OCI layers and
#                           .flatpak bundles actually use
#   openssl                 libarchive's digest support; without libcrypto it
#                           falls back to its own weaker internal hashes
#   libxml2                 xar format reading
#   acl, attr               archived ACLs and extended attributes, which matter
#                           for anything unpacking a system image
#
# --disable-acl and --disable-xattr DO exist and both default to "check", so
# those two are the only members of the set with a lever. They are deliberately
# NOT disabled: an archiver that silently drops extended attributes while
# unpacking a root filesystem is precisely the silent-wrongness shape this
# distribution keeps producing.
#
# THE FOUR COMMAND-LINE TOOLS ARE OFF. bsdtar, bsdcpio, bsdcat and bsdunzip all
# default to ON and are payload -- binaries installed on every system for
# formats GNU tar already handles here. flatpak links the library and never
# invokes them. Turning them off is the same class of decision as libgpg-error's
# Common Lisp binding: the build succeeds either way and nothing about a green
# build says what went into the tarball.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static \
--disable-bsdtar --disable-bsdcpio --disable-bsdcat --disable-bsdunzip"
